---
/**
 * MarkGlyph — the AM lockup, drawn once.
 *
 * His initials on the same 16-unit grid as the rest of the icon system, in one
 * stroke weight (1.5), butt caps, miter joins, `currentColor`.
 *
 * This used to live inside `SiteMark.astro`, and it moved out the day a second
 * surface needed the same drawing: `/design` renders the lockup as a specimen,
 * and `SiteMark.astro` is a fixed link to the home page. Pasting the paths into
 * the design page would have made a fourth copy of a glyph `lib/mark.test.mjs`
 * exists to hold at three, and the copy nobody looks at is always the one that
 * drifts. So the drawing is here and both callers import it. The test reads this
 * file for the same reason it read the old one.
 *
 * The A is the drawing that shipped before the lockup, unchanged to the decimal.
 * The M is built to that A's *ink* metrics rather than its centreline ones,
 * which is the only way two letters drawn with a stroke can share a cap line:
 *
 * - stems flat at 1.7, so the A's mitred apex tip (1.492) stands above them by
 *   1.7% of cap height, the overshoot a point takes over a flat cap in any real
 *   face;
 * - stems flat on 14.1, which is where the A's foot centreline lands;
 * - the valley is the A's apex reflected, the same 55.6 degree included angle
 *   and the same 2.144 miter ratio, with its tip on 14.4498, the A's own lowest
 *   ink.
 *
 * Every diagonal in the mark runs at the A's leg angle, 27.8 degrees off
 * vertical.
 *
 * **Nothing in the mark touches anything.** The crossbar is 0.5 units off the
 * legs and the M is 0.5 units off the A, both measured at the tightest point,
 * which for the letterfit is the outer corner of the A's right foot. That is the
 * rail's own grammar carried into the glyph: the left edge of every page is a
 * stack of free-floating dashes, and this is a monogram held together by
 * proximity rather than by contact. It is also what makes the A separable, which
 * is what the favicon needs.
 *
 * `stroke-miterlimit="2.4"` on the M is the one attribute doing work. The A's
 * apex and the M's valley are 2.144 ratios and stay points; the M's two top
 * corners are 4.163, a 3.1-unit spike out of the top of the box, and are cut
 * flat, which is what a geometric sans does at that junction.
 *
 * **`public/favicon.svg` is the A alone, and that is a measurement rather than a
 * preference.** Rasterised into 16x16 this lockup has *no fully inked pixel at
 * all*: its strokes land at 0.81 device pixels, peak ink 87%, against the A
 * alone's eighteen pixels at 100%. Four other AM constructions were drawn and
 * every one failed the same way; the only one that kept solid ink at 16px bought
 * it by being square and stopped reading as two letters. So the letters live at
 * 20px and up and the favicon keeps the A, which it can, because the A is a
 * component of this lockup rather than a fragment of it.
 *
 * The same geometry is copied into `public/favicon.svg` (the A's two paths) and
 * `scripts/og.mjs` (all three). `lib/mark.test.mjs` compares them and fails on a
 * moved point, on a second stroke weight, or on an M reaching the favicon.
 */
interface Props {
  /**
   * The accessible name, when the glyph is the subject rather than decoration.
   *
   * Omitted, it is hidden: in `SiteMark.astro` the anchor around it already says
   * "Home", and a name here would announce the letters a second time. Given, the
   * glyph becomes an image with that name, which is what `/design` needs, where
   * the drawing is the thing being looked at rather than a way somewhere.
   */
  label?: string;
}

const { label } = Astro.props;
---

{/*
  The box is 29.75 wide against 16 tall. The caller sets `--mark-size`, which is
  the height, because height is the dimension that has to line up with whatever
  the mark sits beside.
*/}
<svg
  class="glyph"
  viewBox="0 0 29.75 16"
  role={label ? "img" : undefined}
  aria-label={label}
  aria-hidden={label ? undefined : "true"}
  focusable="false"
>
  <path
    d="M2.2 14.1 L8 3.1 L13.8 14.1"
    fill="none"
    stroke="currentColor"
    stroke-width="1.5"
    stroke-linejoin="miter"
    stroke-linecap="butt"></path>
  <path
    d="M5.9 10.4 H10.1"
    fill="none"
    stroke="currentColor"
    stroke-width="1.5"
    stroke-linecap="butt"></path>
  <path
    d="M15.713 14.1 L15.713 1.7 L21.588 12.842 L27.463 1.7 L27.463 14.1"
    fill="none"
    stroke="currentColor"
    stroke-width="1.5"
    stroke-linejoin="miter"
    stroke-linecap="butt"
    stroke-miterlimit="2.4"></path>
</svg>

<style>
  /* Height, not width: the box is 29.75 units wide against 16 tall, so the size
     token has to name the one of the two that has to match its neighbours. The
     width follows, which is also what makes a box that stopped matching its
     contents show up as a letterboxed glyph rather than a squashed one. */
  .glyph {
    display: block;
    height: var(--mark-size);
    width: auto;
  }
</style>
